t you could look to  two other countries you could look to  sweden who've had this rational debate  now remember sweden didn't do nothing  they  managed they controlled the disease so  they're in the population it was at  manageable levels  so through the summer they had much  higher cases than we had  when the impact of the disease is low  and actually its impact on healthcare  services is much lower  that's creating population immunity the  only other way to do that is through  vaccination  and it's looking like the vaccination is  not going to be available until the end  of the winter  and even if we do have a vaccination  that we need to forget  some level of population immunity which  is what we do for all other infections  in the community  that's at the same time though at the  same time though the recent studies seem  to suggest that our immunity levels are  pretty low perhaps as you say because we  haven't followed  the model like sweden and obviously this  is a very  deadly disease for older people  vulnerable people  are we really saying that you're asking  people to shield indefinitely what do  they have to go to work are you saying  that they're not allowed to see their  grandchildren  i mean what kind of life is that now  look for instance if you look at germany  germany is a very good example  one of the issues in germany is the over  75 case fatality rate is the same across  europe completely the same it's not like  their health care improves their  outcomes  but what they did is have a strategy to  reduce the number of infections in over  75 year olds  where over 75 of the deaths occur so if  you understand that basic principle  yes right now there is a signal in care  homes  that we need a care on control plan as a  matter of urgency to shield that  population  if you do that well you'll get rid of 40  percent of the mortality  if you improve nosocomial infections  hospital acquired infections  you may get rid of another 10 or 20 well  there you've